  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Sunday)\nIssued at 300 AM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nAs noted in the short term section above, the remnants of the \ntropical disturbance will be feature that could bring a chance of \nshowers/storms across parts of the area with some deterministic \nmodels and ensemble members keeping a remnant vorticity \nmaxima/weakness in the area through Friday. However, it is \nimportant to note that other ensemble members completely dissipate\nthe system before it reaches Oklahoma or western north Texas. \n\nOtherwise, hot weather will likely continue into next weekend \nwith deterministic and ensemble NWP in agreement the subtropical \nridge will continue to influence our weather. As a matter of fact,\nthere are indications the subtropical ridge may further \namplify/strengthen across the Plains by the upcoming weekend. \n\nThe probabilistic National Blend of Models (NBM) forecast does \nnot have any significant spread among the 10th and 90th percentile\ntemperature forecast with even the 10th percentile several \ndegrees above-normal through the Labor Day weekend. As a result, \nthere is a high confidence of generally above-normal temperatures \noutside of any potential tropical influence. Note that given the \nlower sun angle and shorter days, our normal temperatures have \nbeen quickly decreasing over the last couple weeks. For example, \nOklahoma City is down to 89 deg F and Wichita Falls is down to 93 \ndeg F today. Therefore, even a slight cool down would still result\nin above-normal temperatures.\n\nMahale",
